# TypeScript file size: max ~40KB (split instead of grow)

Agents and LLMs need to ingest game-logic modules. Large TypeScript files are hard to edit safely.

## Measure bytes, not lines

The ceiling is **40960 bytes on disk**, not a line count. `oracleIRParser.ts` is ~2.1k lines and ~863 KB of dense regex and literals.

```bash
node tools/ts-logic-file-sizes.mjs            # files over the ceiling
node tools/ts-logic-file-sizes.mjs --all --top 40
```

## Rule

If an addition or edit to any **TypeScript logic** file (especially `server/`, `rules-engine/`, game-logic, resolution, triggers, casts, combat, undo, oracle IR) would push that file **over ~40KB**, do **not** keep growing it.

Instead:

1. Extract the new or related logic into a **new smaller module**
2. Use a **descriptive filename** that hints at contents (e.g. `resolutionLibrarySearchHandlers.ts`, not `utils2.ts`)
3. Keep each resulting file **under ~40KB** when practical
4. Re-export from the parent/barrel when needed so existing imports keep working
5. Prefer cohesive domain splits over tiny one-function files

## Find before you read

Never ingest a megafile to make an edit. Run `node tools/ts-cluster-toc.mjs <file> --min-bytes 2000` or Grep a table of contents first — `^(export )?(async )?function`, `case ResolutionStepType`, `case '` — then Read only the cluster's line range. `docs/mtg-logic-module-map.md` says which module already owns a mechanic.

## Naming

`domain + mechanic + role`, e.g. `resolutionGraveyardSelectionResumeHandlers.ts`, `oracleIRParserPostprocessLookChooseFromTop.ts`.

Forbidden: `utils2.ts`, `helpers.ts`, and `BatchNNN` / `Bulk` ordinals (`oracleIRExecutorConditionalStepSupportBatch368Bulk.ts`).

Every extract starts with a header comment naming its origin parent, the cluster that moved, and the public entry symbols, so agents can grep headers instead of opening the parent.

## Dual barrel: public `X.ts`, internal `X-handlers/index.ts`

| Barrel | Job | Imported by |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Public / compatibility: `X.ts` | Stable path (`./resolution.js`, `./triggered-abilities.js`). Eventually only registration/emit entry points plus named re-exports. Target under 40KB. | The rest of the app, tests |
| Internal / agent TOC: `X-handlers/index.ts` (or the existing `triggers/index.ts`) | Named re-exports of extracted modules, one-line comment each. Under ~10KB so an agent can ingest the whole TOC. | Extracted siblings, tests, agents |

- **Never `X.ts` and `X/index.ts` in the same directory** — resolution is ambiguous. The folder takes a different name: `resolution-handlers/`, `game-actions-handlers/`, `triggers/`. Never create `resolution/` or `game-actions/`.
- Index files hold **named re-exports only**: no `export *` (hides the TOC, reintroduces cycles), no implementations.
- Create the `*-handlers/` folder on **extract #1** of a new mega parent, so we do not accumulate another 35 flat prefixes.
- **Cycle rule:** the public parent may import leaves or the deps factory; a leaf must never import the public parent, nor the index that re-exports it.

## Dual-copy check (before extracting)

Grep the symbol first. If the same export already lives in a folder submodule (this happened with `detectDeathTriggers` / `detectETBTriggers` / `detectAttackTriggers` in `triggered-abilities.ts` vs `triggers/`), **merge into the folder copy and re-export** — do not create a third implementation.

## Temporary parent overshoot

A parent may grow **only** by import/re-export lines while a cluster is being wired, and only if the same session's deleted body more than pays for it. Newly created files must start **under 40KB**. Do not append to a sibling already at ~36–40KB; add a new sibling instead.

## Verify after extracting

Re-read every new or changed import line **plus 2–3 neighbors** in both files for CRLF junk, stray backticks, doubled backslashes, and merged lines (see `.cursor/rules/verify-edited-lines.mdc`), then typecheck the workspace and run targeted tests. Update `docs/mtg-logic-module-map.md` at the end of the session.

Full playbook and paste prompts: `.cursor/skills/split-ts-module/SKILL.md`.

## Applies to

- New features and bug fixes
- Refactors of existing megabytes-scale files (`resolution.ts`, `game-actions.ts`, `oracleIRParserPostprocess.ts`, etc.)
- Both human and agent/Cursor Cloud Agent edits

## Does not require

- Boiling the ocean on unrelated UI/client files unless they are already oversized and you are editing them
- Splitting purely for aesthetics when a file is already under ~40KB

## Soft target

~40KB is the ceiling to respect. Smaller cohesive modules (~10–40KB) are preferred for agent-edited game logic.

## No behavior changes while splitting

Extracts move code. Do not fix cards, rename APIs, or tidy logic in the same edit — a split diff that also changes behavior cannot be reviewed. Do not revive `tools/_refactor_scratch/*.mjs` dump-and-cut scripts as the extraction method; they produced mixed CRLF and reconstruction bugs. Copy bodies with `node tools/extract-ts-cluster.mjs` (byte-copy, `--dry` first). Do not reprint with ts-morph / jscodeshift / recast.

**`appliedSteps` ledger (conditional wrappers):** after a successful `conditional`, push the inner (or else) branch steps only. Push the wrapper itself **only when that branch applied nothing** (`if (result.appliedSteps.length === 0) appliedSteps.push(step)`). Always appending the wrapper after inner steps produced 31 extra-`'conditional'` failures (`['move_zone', 'gain_life']` became `['move_zone', 'gain_life', 'conditional']`) and blew vitest pretty-printing the nested tree. Owner: `oracleIRExecutorMainLoopLateSteps.ts`. Do not “fix” this during an extract; byte-copy the existing `length === 0` guards. Court of Ambition’s discarded/no-life-loss case asserts `['unless_discards']`, not the wrapper.
